--
--
Currently: assertions 2 and 3 FAIL -- the return-type-requirement of a
compound requirement is never satisfied.
Round-90 diagnosis (TWO stacked defects, both located):
1. [temp.constr.atomic]/1: the concept's normalised body must be
   CONTEXTUALLY CONVERTED to bool.  A concept over a trait member
   (`static const bool value`) folds to a c_bool constant, which
   exprt::is_true() does not recognise, so every SATISFIED
   return-type-requirement read as unsatisfied.  Converting the
   constant (as cpp_typecheck_resolve.cpp's tri-state evaluator already
   does for requires-clauses) fixes assertion 3.
2. With that applied, assertion 2 (class with a MEMBER operator-) still
   fails: the concept body `is_int_<decltype((E))>::value` evaluates
   FALSE because the explicit specialization is not selected for the
   substituted type inside the requirement evaluation.
DO NOT land fix 1 alone: it makes previously-(wrongly)-unsatisfied
requirements satisfied and regressed 18 cbmc-cpp tests
(cpp20_vector_*, cpp20_span*, cpp20_ranges_basic, cpp20_erase_if*,
iterator_traits/concept chains) -- the surrounding C++20 library
machinery currently depends on the wrong answer.  Both layers must land
together, ideally with fix 2 first.
